<PageLayout title='Population Genomics' kicker="RESEARCH" lead='Using genomes to reconstruct the movement, divergence and persistence of populations.'>
<section><div class="prose content-page"><p>Population genomics turns variation into historical evidence. Patterns of heterozygosity, allele frequency, linkage and shared ancestry can reveal bottlenecks, expansions, structure, migration and divergence.</p><p>At Homogmos Lab, this line is closely connected to evolutionary genomics and coalescent thinking: the goal is not only to estimate parameters, but to understand which biological histories could have generated the observed genomic pattern.</p><h2>$ approaches</h2><ul><li>Genome-wide diversity and differentiation</li><li>Demographic and coalescent interpretation</li><li>Admixture and introgression questions</li><li>Comparative population histories across genomic compartments</li><li>Reproducible exploratory pipelines in Python, R and Linux</li></ul></div></section>
</PageLayout>